 "facts": {
  "policy": {
   "nbp_reference": 4.0,
   "next_mpc_label": "MPC next: 3-4 Mar",
   "pricing_text": "FRAs at the 25 Feb close (1x4 3.72, 3x6 3.53, 6x9 3.39, 9x12 3.35 vs WIBOR 3M 3.85) price roughly 50bp of NBP cuts within 12 months. The MPC held at 4.00% on 4 Feb; the statement says CPI may fall in Q1 and stay consistent with the target in coming quarters. Glapiński (5 Feb): a March cut if the projection shows nothing worrying; 3.5% possible as the end-point. Glapiński (24 Feb): the March projection shows inflation near target through 2026-27; Litwiniuk (25 Feb): a March cut is probable; Dąbrowski (25 Feb): 3.5% is the end-point. Weak January wages (+6.1%) and industry (-1.5%) on 19 Feb strengthened March pricing (PKO).",
   "risk_text": "A March cut is priced and expected; the risks are the mid-March CPI reweighting, auction fatigue (25 Feb cover 1.32) and Fitch on 27 Feb; oil and US tariffs move the long end."
  },
  "macro": {
   "cpi": {
    "v": 2.2,
    "m": "Jan flash (GUS, 13 Feb; 2025 weights, revision mid-March); Dec 2.4%"
   },
   "projection_text": "NBP November 2025 projection: CPI 2.9% (2026), 2.5% (2027); the March 2026 projection comes with the 3-4 Mar MPC - Glapiński (24 Feb): inflation near target through 2026-27.",
   "fiscal_text": "2025 general-government deficit ~7.0% of GDP; 2026 budget deficit cap PLN 271.7bn (in force; at the Constitutional Tribunal for follow-up review), gross borrowing needs ~PLN 690bn; the Sejm passed the SAFE defence-loan law (13 Feb), and SAFE loans sit outside the Polish public-debt definition (MoF); January central-budget deficit PLN 3.7bn (1.4% of the limit); 2025 central-budget deficit PLN 275.6bn vs 288.8bn planned (MoF, 24 Feb).",
   "fiscal_risk_text": "2026 needs ~38% (25 Feb) financed; budget accounts PLN 183bn at end-January; Fitch reviews Poland on 27 Feb (after the close) and Moody's on 20 Mar, both with negative outlooks.",
   "backdrop_text": "The NBP held at 4.00% on 14 Jan and 4 Feb; January flash CPI 2.2% y/y (13 Feb; 1.9% expected); 2025 GDP grew 3.6% (30 Jan); Q4 +4.0% y/y (12 Feb). Ratings: Fitch A- negative, Moody's A2 negative, S&P A- stable. 25 Feb: the MoF auction sold PLN 11.9bn on only 15.6bn of demand (cover 1.32, the weakest of 2026); ~38% financed; Litwiniuk called a March cut probable, Dąbrowski sees 3.5% as the end-point; GPW Benchmark announced no index changes for 27 Feb; Poland's January HICP 2.5% (new weights). Abroad: euro-area January HICP 1.7%; Greer said the 15% tariff comes 'in coming days'; Bailey sees scope to ease."
  },
  "calendar": {
   "plan_text": "MoF February plan (30 Jan): sale auctions on 11, 18 and 25 Feb (PLN 7-12bn each), no switch auction, a 33-week T-bill auction on 6 Feb; January sales were ~PLN 41.6bn incl. additional sales. BGK placed PLN 1bn of FWA0931 (Armed Forces Support Fund) privately (29-30 Jan). JPY 211.6bn priced on 6 Feb (3Y 1.88%, 5Y 2.22%, 10Y 2.90%, 20Y 3.65%); 33-week T-bills PLN 2.6bn at 3.529%. 11 Feb: PLN 11.9bn sold vs 25.8bn demand (cover 2.18) plus 1.38bn additional; OK0128 unsold; stop yields PS0131 4.298%, DS1033 4.732%, DS1035 5.035%, WS0437 5.148%. 18 Feb: PLN 13.0bn vs 22.1bn (cover 1.70) plus 2.12bn; stop yields OK0128 3.400%, PS0730 4.101%, PS0131 4.212%, DS1034 4.792%, DS1035 4.927%. MoF (20 Feb): February is the last month of WIBOR-linked WZ sales; from March floating supply comes only as POLSTR NZ. 25 Feb: PLN 11.9bn vs 15.6bn (cover 1.32, the weakest of 2026) plus 0.60bn; stop yields PS0131 4.233%, DS0432 4.447%, DS1035 4.947%. February sales ~PLN 40.9bn incl. additional sales; March plan due 27 Feb.",
   "timing_text": "Within each auction week of the Aug 2025-Feb 2026 sample, yields drifted lower into the auction and the auction-day close was the cheapest point relative to the preceding session, so new buying is best executed at the auction-day close (the March dates (plan due 27 Feb)). February covers so far: 2.18x (11 Feb), 1.70x (18 Feb), 1.32x (25 Feb) - demand fading after a heavy start (ING, PKO). The 3-4 Mar MPC with the new projection is the next event; a 25bp cut is the consensus.",
